{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CARrqRmso5sxDotRi6DHMN","type":"tool_result","content":"25-- Neither case is a regression against v1.4.3-rc.1, where both commands were already allowed.\n26-- Minimal fix: return _hit(\"bash_indirect_limit\") when depth > MAX_DEPTH at the top of _scan_segment, and when _normalise uses all 12 passes and still has a launcher as head. Catch RecursionError in scan() and return the same limit hit.\n27-\n28:P1-3. Two parsing defects let a simple prefix skip the scan\n29:Both contradict CHANGELOG line 84 (\"inside sh -c, eval or a here-doc\"). Neither is a regression against the base.\n30-- (a) Where: bash_indirect_guard.py:95-96. Comment stripping treats \"{\", \"}\" and \")\" as the start of a new word. So the \"#\" in ${#var}, ${x}# or $(cmd)# is read as a comment, and the rest of the line is dropped before both _command_substitutions (line 144) and _words (line 101). Example: n=${#list}; sh -c 'rm -rf ~'. The scan sees only \"n=${\" and ALLOWS. The base guard also allows it, because the command word is sh.\n31:- (b) Where: bash_indirect_guard.py:224-227. The heredoc \"consumer\" is taken as the first word of all text since the start or the previous heredoc, not the command that owns the \"<<\". So true && bash <<'EOF' (body rm -rf ~), FOO=1 bash <<EOF, sudo sh <<EOF and cd x; bash <<EOF never have their body scanned. The opaque-stdin check (line ~523) looks for \"<\" in the token list, not \"<<\", so these commands are ALLOWED.\n32:- Minimal fix: treat \"{\" and \"}\" as part of a word, and a \")\" that closes \"$(\" as part of a word. Take the heredoc consumer from the last simple command in the prefix, after _normalise. Treat \"<<\" like \"<\" in the opaque-stdin check. Add tests for both.\n33-\n34-P2 follow-ups\n35-- P2-1. False positives of the heuristic forms (switch-gated; condition 6 says false-positive limits were never measured):"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4310e5bd-b4b6-4a4e-b8bd-71d847027e9a","uuid":"effee51e-8ccd-4ec3-891f-1b7998fad45c","timestamp":"2026-11-16T18:09:35.302Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"25-- Neither case is a regression against v1.4.3-rc.1, where both commands were already allowed.\n26-- Minimal fix: return _hit(\"bash_indirect_limit\") when depth > MAX_DEPTH at the top of _scan_segment, and when _normalise uses all 12 passes and still has a launcher as head. Catch RecursionError in scan() and return the same limit hit.\n27-\n28:P1-3. Two parsing defects let a simple prefix skip the scan\n29:Both contradict CHANGELOG line 84 (\"inside sh -c, eval or a here-doc\"). Neither is a regression against the base.\n30-- (a) Where: bash_indirect_guard.py:95-96. Comment stripping treats \"{\", \"}\" and \")\" as the start of a new word. So the \"#\" in ${#var}, ${x}# or $(cmd)# is read as a comment, and the rest of the line is dropped before both _command_substitutions (line 144) and _words (line 101). Example: n=${#list}; sh -c 'rm -rf ~'. The scan sees only \"n=${\" and ALLOWS. The base guard also allows it, because the command word is sh.\n31:- (b) Where: bash_indirect_guard.py:224-227. The heredoc \"consumer\" is taken as the first word of all text since the start or the previous heredoc, not the command that owns the \"<<\". So true && bash <<'EOF' (body rm -rf ~), FOO=1 bash <<EOF, sudo sh <<EOF and cd x; bash <<EOF never have their body scanned. The opaque-stdin check (line ~523) looks for \"<\" in the token list, not \"<<\", so these commands are ALLOWED.\n32:- Minimal fix: treat \"{\" and \"}\" as part of a word, and a \")\" that closes \"$(\" as part of a word. Take the heredoc consumer from the last simple command in the prefix, after _normalise. Treat \"<<\" like \"<\" in the opaque-stdin check. Add tests for both.\n33-\n34-P2 follow-ups\n35-- P2-1. False positives of the heuristic forms (switch-gated; condition 6 says false-positive limits were never measured):","n