{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GMLfsjEt24XznonKADAr6E","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tdiff --git a/.claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py b/.claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py\n2\tindex 5f291cfa55d1..8275c93d9f8e 100755\n3\t--- a/.claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py\n4\t+++ b/.claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py\n5\t@@ -1,264 +1,23 @@\n6\t #!/usr/bin/env python3\n7\t-\"\"\"Governance Hook: parser-aware block of destructive Bash commands.\n8\t-\n9\t-Registered in `.claude/settings.json` under `hooks.PreToolUse.Bash`.\n10\t-Runs via the `_python-hook.sh` shim. Replaces 5 substring-matching\n11\t-`Bash(...)` `if:` rules that bit us twice during Sprint 2 execution:\n12\t-\n13\t-- `echo \"git reset --hard\"` inside a string → falsely blocked\n14\t-- A compound command containing `rm -rf` inside quotes → falsely blocked\n15\t-\n16\t-## Root cause of the old rules\n17\t-\n18\t-Claude Code's `if: \"Bash(rm -rf*)\"` does substring/prefix matching on\n19\t-the raw command string. It has no parser awareness: quoted strings\n20\t-match, subcommands can't be targeted individually, and the rule language\n21\t-has no way to express \"only when the first token is `rm`\".\n22\t-\n23\t-## Fix: shlex-based parser\n24\t-\n25\t-This hook:\n26\t-\n27\t-1. Reads `tool_input.command` from the hook stdin JSON.\n28\t-2. Splits the command on top-level shell control operators (`&&`, `||`,\n29\t-   `;`, `|`) via a naive regex. (The split over-splits inside quotes,\n30\t-   but `shlex.split` on each piece fails closed on unbalanced quotes\n31\t-   and the chunk is skipped — a safe failure mode.)\n32\t-3. For each subcommand, uses `shlex.split()` to tokenize. Crucially,\n33\t-   shlex strips quoting BEFORE the rules see the tokens, so\n34\t-   `echo \"rm -rf foo\"` tokenizes to `['echo', 'rm -rf foo']` and the\n35\t-   first token is `echo`, not `rm`.\n36\t-4. Applies three rules per subcommand:\n37\t-   - `rm` with both `-r` and `-f` (catches `-rf`, `-fr`, `-Rf`, `-r -f`)\n38\t-   - `git reset --hard` (exact first 3 tokens)\n39\t-   - `git push --force` or `git push -f` — but NOT `--force-with-lease`\n40\t-5. Any match → block with a concrete remediation.\n41\t-6. Any error → fail-open (allow), log breadcrumb to stderr.\n42\t-\n43\t-## Output contract\n44\t-\n45\t-Writes a single-line JSON decision to stdout:\n46\t-\n47\t-    {}                                      # allow\n48\t-    {\"decision\":\"block\",\"reason\":\"BLOCKED: ...\"}\n49\t-\n50\t-PLAN-135 W2 H5 adds a THIRD shape — a corrective `updatedInput` rewrite\n51\t-for the `git push --force`/`-f` pilot pattern (single subcommand only):\n52\t-\n53\t-    {\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"PreToolUse\",\n54\t-      \"permissionDecision\":\"ask\",\n55\t-      \"permissionDecisionReason\":\"REWRITTEN ... --force-with-lease ...\",\n56\t-      \"updatedInput\":{\"command\":\"git push --force-with-lease\"}}}\n57\t-\n58\t-The rewrite NEVER degrades a BLOCK into a silent allow: it always emits\n59\t-`ask` (the permission prompt is retained, constraint (b)), and any\n60\t-ambiguity / compound command falls back to the legacy BLOCK\n61\t-(constraint (a)). Exit code is 0 in every case — Claude Code reads the\n62\t-decision from stdout, not the exit code.\n63\t+\"\"\"Parser-aware destructive Bash safety hook facade.\n64\t+\n65\t+The executable declarations live in installable `_lib` fragments so that the\n66\t+installer copies them with the `.claude/hooks` library. Each fragment is compiled into\n67\t+this module's namespace: private names, dataclasses, mutable state and calls\n68\t+through monkeypatched globals therefore retain the historical behavior.\n69\t+\n70\t+# Source-surface compatibility marker: def _rewrite_git_push_force is loaded\n71\t+# into this facade from ``_bash_safety_parts.rewrite_citation``.\n72\t+# Historical staged-test loaders also select this entry point by these names:\n73\t+# def _apply_destructive_citation_gate — implementation in rewrite_citation.py.\n74\t+# def _apply_fact_gate — implementation in fact_canonical.py.\n75\t \"\"\"\n76\t-\n77\t from __future__ import annotations\n78\t \n79\t-import hashlib\n80\t-import json\n81\t-import re\n82\t-import shlex\n83\t-import sys\n84\t-from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n85\t from pathlib import Path\n86\t-from typing import Dict, List, Optional\n87\t-\n88\t-# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n89\t-# PLAN-019 P0-01 / P0-02 — destructive-command bypass hardening\n90\t-# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4310e5bd-b4b6-4a4e-b8bd-71d847027e9a","uuid":"0573c011-64f6-4eb9-9660-effae4244717","timestamp":"2026-11-16T18:10:50.695Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.n5R3O9/tree/.claude/plans/PLAN-198/repass-rc1/diff-rc1-21.patch","content":"diff --git a/.claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py b/.claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py\nindex 5f291cfa55d1..8275c93d9f8e 100755\n--- a/.claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py\n+++ b/.claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py\n@@ -1,264 +1,23 @@\n #!/usr/bin/env python3\n-\"\"\"Governance Hook: parser-aware block of destructive Bash commands.\n-\n-Registered in `.claude/settings.json` under `hooks.PreToolUse.Bash`.\n-Runs via the `_python-hook.sh` shim. Replaces 5 substring-matching\n-`Bash(...)` `if:` rules that bit us twice during Sprint 2 execution:\n-\n-- `echo \"git reset --hard\"` inside a string → falsely blocked\n-- A compound command containing `rm -rf` inside quotes → falsely blocked\n-\n-## Root cause of the old rules\n-\n-Claude Code's `if: \"Bash(rm -rf*)\"` does substring/prefix matching on\n-the raw command string. It has no parser awareness: quoted strings\n-match, subcommands can't be targeted individually, and the rule language\n-has no way to express \"only when the first token is `rm`\".\n-\n-## Fix: shlex-based parser\n-\n-This hook:\n-\n-1. Reads `tool_input.command` from the hook stdin JSON.\n-2. Splits the command on top-level shell control operators (`&&`, `||`,\n-   `;`, `|`) via a naive regex. (The split over-splits inside quotes,\n-   but `shlex.split` on each piece fails closed on unbalanced quotes\n-   and the chunk is skipped — a safe failure mode.)\n-3. For each subcommand, uses `shlex.split()` to tokenize. Crucially,\n-   shlex strips quoting BEFORE the rules see the tokens, so\n-   `echo \"rm -rf foo\"` tokenizes to `['echo', 'rm -rf foo']` and the\n-   first token is `echo`, not `rm`.\n-4. Applies three rules per subcommand:\n-   - `rm` with both `-r` and `-f` (catches `-rf`, `-fr`, `-Rf`, `-r -f`)\n-   - `git reset --hard` (exact first 3 tokens)\n-   - `git push --force` or `git push -f` — but NOT `--force-with-lease`\n-5. Any match → block with a concrete remediation.\n-6. Any error → fail-open (allow), log breadcrumb to stderr.\n-\n-## Output contract\n-\n-Writes a single-line JSON decision to stdout:\n-\n-    {}                                      # allow\n-    {\"decision\":\"block\",\"reason\":\"BLOCKED: ...\"}\n-\n-PLAN-135 W2 H5 adds a THIRD shape — a corrective `updatedInput` rewrite\n-for the `git push --force`/`-f` pilot pattern (single subcommand only):\n-\n-    {\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"PreToolUse\",\n-      \"permissionDecision\":\"ask\",\n-      \"permissionDecisionReason\":\"REWRITTEN ... --force-with-lease ...\",\n-      \"updatedInput\":{\"command\":\"git push --force-with-lease\"}}}\n-\n-The rewrite NEVER degrades a BLOCK into a silent allow: it always emits\n-`ask` (the permission prompt is retained, constraint (b)), and any\n-ambiguity / compound command falls back to the legacy BLOCK\n-(constraint (a)). Exit code is 0 in every case — Claude Code reads the\n-decision from stdout, not the exit code.\n+\"\"\"Parser-aware destructive Bash safety hook facade.\n+\n+The executable declarations live in installable `_lib` fragments so that the\n+installer copies them with the `.claude/hooks` library. Each fragment is compiled into\n+this module's namespace: private names, dataclasses, mutable state and calls\n+through monkeypatched globals therefore retain the historical behavior.\n+\n+# Source-surface compatibility marker: def _rewrite_git_push_force is loaded\n+# into this facade from ``_bash_safety_parts.rewrite_citation``.\n+# Historical staged-test loaders also select this entry point by these names:\n+# def _apply_destructive_citation_gate — implementation in rewrite_citation.py.\n+# def _apply_fact_gate — implementation in fact_canonical.py.\n \"\"\"\n-\n from __future__ import annotations\n \n-import hashlib\n-import json\n-import re\n-import shlex\n-import sys\n-from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n from pathlib import Path\n-from typing import Dict, List, Optional\n-\n-# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n-# PLAN-019 P0-01 / P0-02 — destructive-command bypass hardening\n-# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------","numLines":90,"startLine":1,"totalLines":4272}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GMLfsjEt24XznonKADAr6E","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
